+# Report — Prompt 004: CS2 Odyssey gates and rubrics, Weeks 7–17
+
+## Built
+
+- **Weeks 7–8:** plain Quick Check gates/rubrics for inheritance then actual polymorphism. Week 7 requires an is-a hierarchy, `super().__init__`, and a behavior override. Week 8 requires a common method call over mixed subclasses with different real effects.
+- **Week 9:** Checkpoint 2 gate/rubric using the four full-Build axes (Functions, Concept use, Explanation, Demonstrability) plus an inheritance Debrief.
+- **Weeks 10–11:** recursion gates/rubrics. Both require a reachable base case, shrinking recursive case, and a live trace with more than one recursive level. Week 11 adds Decide/Compare #1: recursion versus iteration defended for the student's actual feature.
+- **Week 12:** a plot of accumulated world data, readable labels, and a data-grounded observation.
+- **Week 13:** real-data search trace, meaningful sort, and accurate O(n) linear-search / sorted-data-for-binary-search reasoning.
+- **Week 14:** recursion-plus-plotting Checkpoint 3, four-axis Build rubric, and Full Trail Debrief.
+- **Week 16:** searching/sorting/full-semester Checkpoint 4, four-axis Build rubric, capstone Decide/Compare, and capstone Debrief.
+- **Week 17:** created `assignments/A5-final-reflection.md` and `planning/week-17-finals.md`, because Prompt 002’s counterparts were not yet present. The reflection closes the Debrief across CS1 foundations and CS2 topics.
+
+## Grounding
+
+Technical requirements are grounded in the supplied Deitel PDF: its inheritance/base-class/subclass and polymorphism treatment; recursion and recursion-versus-iteration treatment; Matplotlib/data-visualization material; and search, sort, binary-search precondition, and Big-O treatment. The supplied PDF’s table of contents numbers those topics differently from the prompt’s course mapping (notably inheritance appears in book Chapter 10 and recursion/search/sort/Big-O in Chapter 11); files cite topic/section rather than asserting the conflicting number.
+
+## Depth beyond the CS1 baseline
+
+CS2 asks for reuse via `super()`, meaningful overrides, demonstrated polymorphism, multi-level live recursion, a defended recursion/iteration decision, world-data visualization, and algorithm-growth reasoning. Checkpoints require an integrated runnable demonstration, explanation, and demonstrability rather than only a feature’s existence.
+
+## Parallel assumptions and open questions
+
+- Prompt 002’s report and skeleton files were absent when this work began. The CS1 four-genre menu was retained: Frontier Settlement, Investigation Bureau, Starship Log, and Small Business.
+- Week 15 is assumed to be the CS1-style buffer/async week with no gate. No Week 15 file was created.
+- CS1 exemplar Week 9 still states an older two-axis checkpoint convention, but this prompt explicitly requires a four-axis Build rubric; the CS2 Week 9 rubric follows the confirmed prompt and the toolkit’s four axes.
+- The task’s chapter numbers conflict with the supplied PDF’s contents. Topic mapping was followed exactly; this needs only a curriculum-label check before publication, not a redesign.
+
